<!--
  The three shaders this mod ships, declared as ShaderTypeDefs so they are reachable the way
  vanilla's own are (Data/Core/Defs/Misc/ShaderTypeDefs/ShaderTypes.xml is the file this mirrors,
  down to the folder name).

  WHY A DEF RATHER THAN THE LITERAL PATH STRING THE C# USED TO CARRY. Three reasons, in the order
  they matter here:

    1. A def is reachable from XML and a string in our assembly is not. GraphicData.shaderType,
       PawnRenderNodeProperties.shaderTypeDef, TerrainDef.customShader and HediffDef.skinShader all
       take a ShaderTypeDef, so anything that should ever render through one of these — ours or
       another mod's patch — has no other route. None of the three is used that way today; the point
       is that the door is open without an assembly reference on us.
    2. A missing defName is a RED STARTUP ERROR from DefOfHelper, whereas a mistyped path string is a
       yellow warning somewhere in the middle of Player.log next to a silent fallback. This mod has
       already paid for one silent shader failure (the white slabs that CloudVolumeShader's ShaderName
       check exists to catch), and loud beats clever.
    3. It puts the path in the same kind of file as the rest of the mod's content, so publish.sh's
       CONTENT_DIRS staging covers it automatically.

  THE PATHS BELOW ARE RELATIVE TO THE BUNDLE'S Materials FOLDER, not to anything on disk. RimWorld
  builds the full asset path as Assets/Data/<packageId>/Materials/<shaderPath>.shader, and
  Tools/ShaderBundle/Project/Assets/Editor/BuildShaderBundles.cs is what puts the .shader files
  there. The two have to change together and nothing checks that they agree at build time — which is
  what Tests/CelestialLighting.Tests/ShaderTypeDefTests.cs is for.

  NO <uiShaderPath> ON ANY OF THESE. It exists for shaders that need a separate variant when drawn
  into the UI rather than onto the map; all three of ours are map passes with no UI counterpart, and
  naming a path that does not exist in the bundle would log a warning per shader on every boot.
-->
<Defs>

  <!-- The volumetric cloud march (§25c). Falls back to §25b's baked atlas when unavailable. -->
  <ShaderTypeDef>
    <defName>CL_CloudVolume</defName>
    <shaderPath>CelestialCloudVolume</shaderPath>
  </ShaderTypeDef>

  <!-- Vector lighting's max(vanilla, ours) composition (§27). Falls back to the crossfade. -->
  <ShaderTypeDef>
    <defName>CL_VectorLightMax</defName>
    <shaderPath>VectorLightMax</shaderPath>
  </ShaderTypeDef>

  <!-- The aurora curtain's field, evaluated per fragment (§11a). Falls back to the CPU bake. -->
  <ShaderTypeDef>
    <defName>CL_Aurora</defName>
    <shaderPath>CelestialAurora</shaderPath>
  </ShaderTypeDef>

</Defs>
